WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.409
A complex input has two real directions

00:00:03.409 --> 00:00:10.718
Squaring one plus i gives two i because the real terms cancel and the cross terms add

00:00:10.718 --> 00:00:17.167
Complex differentiability asks for one limiting multiplier that works for changes approaching from every direction.

00:00:18.467 --> 00:00:24.325
For a nonzero complex change h, expand the numerator and cancel its factor h

00:00:24.325 --> 00:00:27.254
The quotient becomes two z plus h

00:00:27.254 --> 00:00:34.367
Its limit is two z regardless of the direction of approach, so squaring is complex differentiable everywhere.

00:00:35.667 --> 00:00:42.263
Complex conjugation looks smooth as a real reflection, but fails this stronger derivative test

00:00:42.263 --> 00:00:45.561
Along real changes, the quotient is one

00:00:45.561 --> 00:00:49.331
Along purely imaginary changes, it is negative one

00:00:49.331 --> 00:00:53.100
Different directional limits rule out a complex derivative.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:01:00.467
Writing the function as u plus i v produces the Cauchy Riemann equations

00:01:00.467 --> 00:01:07.933
Continuous first partial derivatives satisfying these equations throughout an open region are sufficient for holomorphicity there

00:01:07.933 --> 00:01:13.067
Checking equations at an isolated point without regularity is not enough.
